my name is Eric Jarvis I'm an associate  professor at Duke University Medical  Center I'm also a Howard Hughes Medical  Institute investigator and I'm going to  be talking to you today about why I  became a scientist and how I became a  scientist just to let you know I right  now I run a laboratory that runs roughly  has anywhere from 15 to 20 persons  depending on the semester and we study  brain pathways for vocal learning but  that's not how I begin so I grew up in  New York City mostly in the Bronx and  queens and I grew up in a family that  came from diverse economic backgrounds  from being relatively poor middle class  and we always were told you know to have  a dream in life to make your life better  to do something important but most of my  family was into the performing arts  particularly on my mother's side and so  we were all dreaming to be you know  famous artists singers and dancers and  so forth and you know to just do this  with a passion and so when I was a  youngster in elementary school and  eventually going to high school I  auditioned for the High School of  Performing Arts and got to be a dance  major there and I then also was at the  Joffrey Ballet School in New York City  and the Alvin Ailey Dance Company and I  was pushing along to be a serious dancer  and I would dance six hours a day of  three hours at school three hours at one  of these dance schools and in between  would have academic classes like you  know math biology reading writing and so  on some point towards the end of my high  school years I started really thinking  about you know is this the career I want  to pursue and I was doing okay and for  instance in my senior year towards the  end of that year I was asked to audition  for  Alvin Ailey Dance School I mean the the  the company and I was asking myself what  do I really want to do in life and I  started asking like that question  because my mother always said to me it  doesn't really matter what you do what's  most important is that what you do you  do it well and she would then add but  I'd like you to do something that has a  positive impact on society and I thought  you know what can I do that has a  positive impact on society and I felt I  could do that better as a scientist then  I could do that as a dancer so so in my  senior dance concert right at the end of  the dance concert I'm thinking making  this decision here and I said you know  what I'd like to go ahead and be a  scientist and I didn't have any  scientific background training at all I  had biology classes which I really liked  so I applied to different colleges got  into Hunter college and actually you had  to take remedial courses because they  said I wasn't prepared enough to take  the advanced academic classes so I took  a summer of remedial classes and then  really started intensively studying  biology and mathematics like I was  studying dance and the reason why I was  studying biology and math is because I  felt I wasn't sure if I really liked  biology better or let's say that is the  understanding of how biological  organisms work or the origins of the  universe those are my two major interest  so I decided to do both and I thought  math would actually helped me think  better so then after my first year I got  into a program that helps  underrepresented minorities get into  scientific research and this was a great  advantage that I discovered later offset  a disadvantage that I had that I didn't  realize many other people had and then I  really worked with a passion in the lab  I would sometimes spend a night in the  lab do experiments on the weekends on  the holidays and people calling up where  is Eric where is Eric where can we find  him in the lab that's where he was and I  fell in love with doing scientific  research in the lab like I did as a  dancer and people often asked  well how how did you make that switch  aren't those two different fields very  different and I discovered at that time  no they're not both of them require a  lot of discipline both of them require a  lot of hard work both of them require  acceptance of failure that is you have  to do a choreographed dance over and  over and over again until you get it  right just like an experiment sometimes  if you submit a paper for a publication  it gets rejected sometimes you go for an  edition you get rejected so you have to  learn how to accept rejection and try  over again and importantly both of them  require a good level of creativity as an  artist you need to be creative to have  your art be appreciated by others you  need to come up with something new same  thing for science you need to be  creative to understand how nature works  and so to me dance and science was a  natural merge of two different fields  then when I was at Hunter College where  I did my undergraduate scientific  research and also academic training I  decided what do I want to do  particularly within science for my  future career and I decided you know  what I really am interested in two  questions one is the origins of the  universe and the other is how the brain  works and in trying to choose between  those two I chose I to study how the  brain works  why because I felt it was more rooted in  my dance background that is more wooden  in something here in the earth and as  result of that I applied to different  graduate schools and I got into a number  of them because from all the hard work I  did as an undergraduate student I was  able to publish six papers in scientific  journals either as you know first author  papers or collaborative papers with  others in the lab and that helped me  become competitive to get into some good  graduate school programs I was trying to  decide do I want to do an MD PhD or a  to answer this question about brain  function and I decided on the PhD  because usually the PhDs are the folks  who actually make the discoveries to  understand how things work or the cures  for diseases so I went to Rockefeller  University as a graduate student and I  began to study the songbird brain and  discovered genes that are regulated in  those areas that either control the  neural circuits for forming learned  vocalizations or at least producing them  and to make a long story short to skip a  number of years later to now being a  associate professor at Duke University  Medical Center teaching medical students  on how the brain works I recently  discovered in with my collaborators that  the brain pathways that control vocal  learning abilities are embedded within  brain areas that control motor sequence  behavior including the potentially  learning how to dance and so now I'm  actually coming full circle to bringing  my scientific career and my former dance  training together because those two  brain circuits seem to be related to  each other and I hope in the future to  discover what that relationship really  is and I think the answer is going to  tell us how brain pathways for complex  circuits evolve in general and this  would be a natural nice merging of art  and science  you
 

